From 5445b783c2b9297be8f9306932fa17839a10f01d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leonardo Tada Antunes <leonardotada@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 13:29:30 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] cards: one window for the collector's memory, a range-checked
 barrier ABI

The card table indexed by absolute address needed 256 GiB (+2 GiB of summary)
of address space, a table set up from .preinit_array (shared libraries do not
get it, and an Android game is one), and it could not tell a heap address from
a malloc pointer with a tag in its top byte.

The memory that holds the program's pointers now comes from one contiguous
window reserved when the collector starts, before main (createProcess), 256
GiB on a desktop and 16 GiB on Android and iOS, SYMGC_HEAP_RESERVE_GIB to
change it, halved down to 2 GiB if the system will not give it. The card table
covers the window only (1/512 of it). The barrier is range checked against
`__sd_gc_card_lo` and `__sd_gc_card_n`, zero until the collector starts, which
makes it a no-op until then: nothing has to run before the first constructor,
so the .preinit_array / __mod_init_func / .CRT$XCU hooks are gone.

collectCards and __sd_gc_card_range work on indices relative to the window; the
Windows commit and decommit of table pages go through cardsCommit and
cardsDecommit (and decommit only the pages that hold nothing but the purged
cards).

Compiling the whole collector for other targets (ldc2 -mtriple=..., compile
only) found what had never been compiled outside Linux, now fixed: the Windows
constants imported from the wrong module; usleep and a scoping error in code
written for Linux only (a portable sleepMicros, and a VirtualAlloc for the root
chunks); sched_getcpu, which Android only has from API 29 (the arena comes from
the thread id there). x86_64-windows-msvc, aarch64-linux-android and
aarch64-linux-gnu now compile with the card barrier; macOS and iOS still need a
waiter and a stop-the-world protocol, written up in guide/platforms.md.

Checked (Linux x86-64, LDC with the range-checked barrier): mark_race (21.8M
moves, 0 violations; the same program without the barrier loses ~80k per run),
finalizer_order, phobos_stress x3, thread_exit_race, the 113 unit tests, DMD
with the kernel tracker unchanged, and cards_window (default window, a 4 GiB
window with the whole table summed, and a 32 GiB address-space limit that makes
the collector settle for 16 GiB).

